TI  - Effects of Flipped, Web-Based, and Traditional Learning Models on Clinical Nurses’ Self-Directed Learning and Metacognition
AU  - Emine Akçin Şenyuva
AU  - Deniz Yildirim
JO  - CIN Computers Informatics Nursing
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1097/cin.0000000000001464
AN  - 41488981
AB  - Global health crises, armed conflicts, and technological advancements such as artificial intelligence have made nursing care more complex, requiring clinical nurses to develop stronger self-directed learning and metacognitive thinking skills. However, most educational interventions focus on nursing students, with limited attention given to clinical nurses and the use of innovative learning methods. This study examined the effects of evidence-based practice training delivered through flipped learning, web-based learning, and traditional classroom methods on clinical nurses' self-directed learning and metacognitive thinking. A randomized controlled trial was conducted with 45 clinical nurses in a training and research hospital. Participants were randomly assigned to 1 of 3 groups: flipped learning, web-based learning, or traditional instruction. Data were collected using validated scales before the intervention, immediately after, and 1 month later. Findings indicated that both flipped and web-based learning significantly improved nurses' self-directed learning and metacognitive thinking skills compared to traditional learning. No significant difference was found between the flipped and web-based models. These results suggest that innovative, flexible learning approaches can enhance evidence-based practice capabilities among clinical nurses and should be integrated into continuing education programs to improve quality of care in clinical settings.
